WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.390
The ministry sent us a table:

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.990
attendance and marks for every

3
00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.430
class, for a whole year.

4
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.440
My teachers asked: where do we even start?

5
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.450
We needed a routine.

6
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:16.890
Today: a five-question routine for

7
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.670
exploring any table of data.

8
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.480
First, what is in it?

9
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.440
Count the rows and columns.

10
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.720
What is one row: a pupil,

11
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.940
a class or a day?

12
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.430
Which columns are numbers, and

13
00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.330
which are categories?

14
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.280
Second, the shape.

15
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.500
Draw a histogram of each number column.

16
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.610
Is it a hill, or does it have a long tail?

17
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.020
Third, the centre and the spread.

18
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.470
Give the median and the range for skewed

19
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.990
columns, and the mean and the standard

20
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.790
deviation for symmetric ones.

21
00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.610
Fourth, gaps and oddities.

22
00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.650
Look for missing values, outliers and

23
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.750
impossible numbers, like a class with one

24
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.250
hundred and twenty percent attendance.

25
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.210
Fifth, groups and relationships.

26
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.130
Compare boys and girls, morning

27
00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:14.670
and afternoon, or villages.

28
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:16.940
Draw a scatter plot of

29
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.020
attendance and marks.

30
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.350
Then ask the next question.

31
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.080
Quick check!

32
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.150
One class shows one hundred and

33
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.670
twenty percent attendance.

34
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.970
Which step of the routine catches this?

35
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.750
Gaps and oddities.

36
00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.430
Attendance can't pass one hundred

37
00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.670
percent, so something went wrong,

38
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:39.970
maybe pupils counted twice.

39
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.370
Fix or flag it before you compare classes.

40
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.050
Then write three findings,

41
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.370
each with evidence.

42
00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.010
For example: attendance is lowest

43
00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.230
in the rainy months, with a median

44
00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.010
of eighty percent in July.

45
00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.030
A finding needs a number and

46
00:01:59.030 --> 00:01:59.890
where it came from.

47
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:01.660
Your turn.

48
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.730
Which is the better finding: attendance

49
00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:08.230
is bad, or attendance falls to a median

50
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:09.670
of eighty percent in July?

51
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.820
The second one: it has a

52
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:13.980
number and a time.

53
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.990
Remember the routine: what is in it,

54
00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.450
shape, centre and spread, gaps and

55
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.870
oddities, groups and relationships.

56
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.940
Then write findings with evidence.

57
00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.610
Try it in class tomorrow: give

58
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.790
groups a small school table and

59
00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.330
the five questions on a card.

60
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.480
Each group writes three findings with

61
00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.080
evidence and one next question.
